[EncDescription_data/units/flak/rus/37mm_61_k]
37mm Automatic Air Defense Gun M1939 (61-K)

Soviet development of an automatic air defense gun had been in the works since the 1920s, but was fraught with problems and delays. In 1926-28, the design bureau of the Bolshevik Factory No. 232 created an improved variant of the 40mm Vickers gun, which was adopted as the M1928 37mm automatic air defense gun. But in 1929-33, Factory No. 8  attempted to launch mass production of the gun, but failed. In 1930, the USSR signed a contract with Rheinmetall, for shipping blueprints and artillery samples, including the 20mm and 37mm automatic air defense guns (specifically, the 20mm automatic air defense gun M1930 (2-K) and the 37mm automatic air defense gun M1930 (4-K)). In 1932-33, the Factory No. 8 attempted to begin mass production of these guns as well, but only managed to build 64 of these 20mm guns, and they were generally agreed to be low quality: only 31 pieces of which still remained in active service by November 1, 1936. Mass production of the 37mm gun had failed to launch yet again. Meanwhile, Germany had adopted these same guns, as the 20mm FlaK 30 and 37mm FlaK 18.
In 1932-38, the design bureau, under the supervision of M. N. Kondakov, built a number of air defense gun prototypes, but none of these were adopted. There were yet further unsuccessful attempts to create other air defense guns based on aircraft guns. Eventually the decision was made to solicit foreign expertise once more. As a result, in 1937, Factory No. 8 blueprinted the 45mm automatic gun which became the 49-K, based on an air defense autocannon by Bofors. The prototype passed tests and was finally adopted in 1939. However, even this gun never entered mass production, because the military command considered the 37mm caliber too large for an automatic air defense gun.
But in 1938, another 37mm automatic air defense gun based on the 49-K was being developed. It was adopted in 1939 and had entered mass production by the end of the year.
This gun, known as the 61-K, was a classic automatic air defense gun carried by a four-wheel vehicle and converted into the combat mode by means of four supports. It used a five-round clip and offered convenient maintenance and high reliability. On the downside, faulty clip feeding could result in slower firing rate. The ammunition included both fragmentation and armor-piercing rounds. The gun’s excellent ballistic qualities allowed its use against enemy tanks.
Until December of 1942, these 37mm guns were used by rifle divisions – initially as artillery battalions, then as batteries. Not nearly enough of these guns were manufactured to meet demand, so most divisions did not benefit from automatic air defense guns. The majority of these pieces were organized as anti-aircraft artillery regiments, which included 12-16 guns each. 61-K guns were used primarily in army air defense, but occasionally in strategic air defense as well, and were sometimes mounted on armored trains and ships. In the beginning of the war, the 37mm air defense gun was also used by anti-tank detachments. Mass production of over the years 1939-45 totaled over 18 thousand 61-K guns. From 1945 forward, the 61-K gun was mounted on the SU-37 self-propelled artillery unit.
Numerous 61-K guns were supplied to North Korea, and they saw extensive use in the Korean War. The gun established a good reputation, and over its service history it was responsible for bringing down large numbers of US aircraft. During the postwar years, the 61-K gun was exported to tens of countries to be used in many armed conflicts. In some places they still remain in service to this day.

[EncDescription_data/units/flak/rus/85mm_52_k]
85mm Air Defense Gun M1939 (52-K)

The history of this gun begins in 1930, when the USSR signed a contract with the German company Rheinmetall for a shipment of the latest artillery blueprints and samples. Included in the contract was the shipment of four 76mm air defense guns, along with the technology necessary for their manufacture. In 1931, after many field tests, the gun was adopted and entered mass production as the M1931 (3-K) 76mm air defense gun. In 1938, it underwent improvements (the barrel design was changed, and the platform replaced), and was re-designated the M1938 76mm air defense gun. However, by that time it had become clear that despite the gun’s improvements, its power was insufficient against newer military aircraft.
In 1937 work had already begun on a new 85mm gun. It was quite closely based on the lower-caliber 3-K, which was held as the finest air defense gun of its time. The new artillery piece entered field tests in 1938, initially mounted on the 3-K carriage, and later on the M1938 gun carriage. It was adopted in 1939 and entered mass production at the Kalinin Machine-Building Plant.
In terms of design, it was a classic air defense gun with good ballistic qualities, a semi-automatic breechblock, high angular elevation, and all-round excellent firepower. A well-trained crew could convert the gun into the combat mode in under two minutes. An overall success, the design was further improved throughout the war. Already by 1941, a special anti-tank variant was being built – the M1941 85mm anti-tank gun. Compared with its counterpart, this gun had a simpler elevating mechanism (for a maximum elevation of 25 degrees), sights and gun shield, no variable recoil mechanism, an anti-aircraft fire director and setter dials, and only one gunner. In 1942, these guns received gun shields (which were later improved). The list of changes also included various barrel types, breechblock semi-automatics and different platforms. But as early as 1944 the gun had already become obsolete – mainly due to its insufficient vertical range – and in the summer of 1945 was replaced by another 85mm air defense gun, the M1944 (KS-1), which had better ballistic qualities.
Most of these guns were used by anti-aircraft regiments, each of which included 16 of these guns. Some of those served as strategic air defense on the home front, while other served as tactical air defense, protecting river crossings, depots, airfields, and other places of troop concentration. Thanks to the gun's good armor penetration ability, it was actively used against tanks, although it was too large and expensive for a true anti-tank piece. In 1941, the 85mm guns were used by anti-tank defensive artillery brigades. Later, they served as standalone tank-destroyer artillery divisions, numbering 12 guns each, which played an important role in the Battle of Kursk. Of all the standard-issue anti-tank units, only 85mm guns could deal with the improved German Pz IV tanks and StuG III assault guns, which carried 80mm frontal armor, not to mention the Tiger heavy tanks which carried the same armor plates on their sides. These standalone tank-destroyer artillery divisions, equipped with 85mm guns, were in active use until 1944, when they were replaced with SU-85 self-propelled tank destroyers, armed – similarly to T-34-85 tanks – with guns tailored to the ballistic qualities of the 85mm gun. The mass production over the years 1939-45 totaled 13422 M1939 85mm air defense guns.
The 52-K gun was also used in the Korean War, mainly to protect the home front from frequent US air raids. The gun established a good reputation, although its fire was somewhat inefficient, in part because inexperienced crews who did not know how to calibrate its fire directors, but also because the existing fire directors were never intended for the newer fighter aircraft.

[EncDescription_data/units/flak/rus/zis_5_37mm_61_k]
This antiaircraft gun was developed in 1939 and, together with the 52K 85mm antiaircraft gun, it served as the main weapon that fought enemy aviation throughout World War II. The gun rewuired little maintenance and had a very reliable design. Its main drawback was a difficulty to reload due to the fact that the inlet was in constant motion together with the barrel. A four-wheel mounting with motor-vehicles enabled a transportation speed of up to 60 km/h. The AZP-37-1 antiaircraft sight was calibrated to compute lateral and vertical deflection angles automatically for targets except flying with a speed of up to 540 km/h. Since 1943 all 61-K guns were equipped with a gun shield to protect the crew of five.

[EncDescription_data/units/flak/usa/40mm_Bofors_AA_Gun]
40mm Automatic Gun M1 (Bofors 40mm) 

The US Army was well aware that small-caliber automatic air defense guns were the most efficient means against attack aircraft, fighter-bombers, and divers. They had adopted the 37mm M2A1 gun as far back as 1927, but it only entered mass production in 1938 due to the economic consequences of the Great Depression. The M2A1 was produced throughout the war, but the US military was not satisfied with it for a number of reasons.
By 1933, the Swedish company Bofors had produced an excellent 40mm automatic air defense gun which quickly became very popular. A number of countries placed orders for the piece, with which they equipped both their warships and their ground troops. These guns were adopted by Poland, Finland, Norway and other countries, and in 1937, they were put to the test in Great Britain, and acknowledged as the best of all artillery pieces of the time. The British purchased a license to manufacture the gun, but mass production was more difficult than expected, particularly because the British used imperial units, while the Swedes used the metric system. The British variant of the gun was known as QF 40mm Mark III.
The US received the Bofors from Great Britain, and after a series of tests, they purchased a number of pieces directly from Sweden, along with a license for mass production. Having become the primary manufacturer of the Bofors gun in the US, Chrysler upgraded the gun considerably, mainly to improve its manufacturability. The gun entered mass production in the US in 1941.
Without a doubt, the Bofors was a very good gun as testified by its widespread use. Even the Soviet 37mm 61-K gun was based on the Swedish design. The Bofors had good ballistic qualities, a high rate of fire, and was reliable and easy to use. The gun mainly used fragmentation rounds, but it also accepted armor-piercing shells, which were capable of dispatching moderately armored enemy vehicles.

The US infantry and armored divisions had no air defense guns, which were separately handled by standalone battalions attached to divisions. The 40mm Automatic Gun M1 saw very active service in North Africa, Italy, Europe and in the Pacific, and during the postwar years, the M1 had a long service history with the US Army, who brought numerous units to the Korean War. However, because the US had a clear air superiority in that conflict, these guns were used primarily against ground targets.

[EncDescription_data/units/flak/ger/88mm_Flak_36_37]
8.8cm FlugabwehrKanone 36 Anti-Aircraft Gun

The development of the most famous German flak gun of the war started in 1928. Designers from the Krupp company worked on their design at the Bofors factory in Sweden, due to the Treaty of Versailles prohibiting Germany from internally developing anti-aircraft guns. The prototype for the weapon was an existing 75mm AA gun. By 1931, the work also proceeded in Germany in secret, where first prototypes were built and secretly tested. The weapon was accepted for service in 1933, designated FlaK 18 (the number 18 meant that the gun was supposedly designed in 1918, before the armistice). 

The weapon was modified in 1935, with the new variant being easier and cheaper to manufacture. The barrel and the carriage were modified, brass parts were replaced with steel, and front and rear axle and wheels were unified. The new variant was accepted for service as FlaK 36. Innovations didn’t stop there. Soon a FlaK 37 variant was introduced, differing mainly in fire direction equipment. Ballistically, all 88 variants were identical, and they were nearly identical externally. 

Technically, the FlaK 36 was a powerful gun with a semiautomatic breech, which offered a high rate of fire. The cross-shaped gun carriage provided for 360 degrees of traverse. The weapon could be transported on two separable axles (front and rear). Some weapons had an armoured shield protecting the crew.

The FlaK 36, together with other variants of the 88, was actively used throughout the war on all fronts, with first action recorded as early as the Spanish Civil War. Organizationally, most of the weapons belonged to the Luftwaffe, although some were sent to the Wehrmacht as well. Most guns were attached to special flak battalions, but some were attached to panzer and panzergrenadier divisions. For example, the flak battalion of the Grossdeutschland Division had twelve 88's in active service. 

From very early on the 88 was often used against ground targets, including armour, and it is in this capacity that the weapon became most famous. Due to the weapon’s excellent performance, it could confidently engage heavy Allied tanks, against which standard anti-tank guns of the Wehrmacht were ineffective. However, the use of a flak gun in an anti-tank role was not without its drawbacks. The 88 was very large and heavy, which made it difficult to transport, set up and camouflage. It was also much more expensive than dedicated anti-tank weapons (for example, it was almost three times as expensive as the 75mm PaK40 anti-tank gun). As a flak gun, the FlaK 36 was mediocre, becoming obsolete during the war. It could not reach high-altitude allied bombers, which led to the development of the FlaK 41 with better altitude performance.

Variants of the "acht-acht" (eight-eight) remained in production throughout the war. 2,459 FlaK 18 and FlaK 36 guns were available on September 1st, 1939; and 18,259 more were manufactured by the end of the war. A KwK 36 tank gun was also developed based on the FlaK 36, which was used in the heavy Tiger tank.

Captured 88mm guns were actively used by the Red Army.


[EncDescription_data/units/flak/ger/SdKfz_6_2]
37mm FlaK 36 auf Fahrgestell Zugkraftwagen 5t Sd Kfz 6/2 Self-Propelled Flak Vehicle

A 5-ton Sd Kfz 6 half-track, designed as a prime mover for the 105mm field howitzer, was one of the vehicles used as a base for self-propelled anti-aircraft guns. The vehicle’s redesign included an installation of a special platform in the back, on which a 37mm automatic FlaK 36 cannon was set up. Sides and rear of the platform could be opened to allow the weapon to fire. The installation allowed for 360 degrees of horizontal coverage. Some vehicles also had armoured shields to protect the crew. A special two-wheel trailer was used to transport ammunition. 

Sd Kfz 6/2 remained in production from 1941 to 1943, with a total of 339 vehicles produced. The vehicle was then replaced by the Sd Kfz 7/2 which housed the same gun, but was based on an 8-ton half-track. The vehicle was mainly used against Allied fighter-bombers and Soviet Il-2 Sturmoviks. When needed, the vehicle was also effective against ground targets. Due to excellent weapon performance, the Sd Kfz 6/2 was very effective against lightly armoured vehicles.


[EncDescription_data/units/flak/ger/SdKfz_7_1]
20-mm Self-Propelled Quad Flak Vehicle 2cm Flakvierling 38 auf Fahrgestell Zugkraftwagen 8t Sd Kfz 7/1

The German High Command placed high priority on self-propelled anti-aircraft vehicles, intended to accompany fast-moving panzer and mechanized infantry divisions. These vehicles were usually based on existing half-track designs, being off-road capable, and cheaper than tank hulls. The Sd Kfz 7 half-track was no exception, becoming a basis for both a 20mm and a 37mm self-propelled AAA. 

The vehicle consisted of a quad 20mm Flakvierling 38 flak gun installed on an open platform in the back of an SdKfz 7. The gun was operated manually, and was fully traversable. When moving, the platform was protected by low side panels, which were lowered when the gun was fired. A new variant was also introduced in 1942, with an armoured cabin and an armoured plate protecting the engine. Some late models also received additional armour for the gun. A special trailer was used to transport ammunition.

Altogether, the vehicle was quite successful, having a good combination of mobility and volume of fire. The SdKfz 7/1 was mainly used against Allied fighter-bombers and Soviet Il-2 Sturmoviks. When needed, the vehicle was also effective against ground targets, although the lack of effective armour made such forays risky. 

The vehicle remained in production from April of 1940 until the end of 1944, with production numbers significantly increasing in 1942. The vehicles were actively used on all fronts. By March 1st of 1945, 319 Sd Kfz 7/1s remained in active service.

[EncDescription_data/units/flak/ger/flak_1_38]
Flak-38 AA gun was developed by Mauser werke as a replacement for Flak-30 AA gun which was obsolete by the end of 1930s because of low fire rate. New gun was used in the army and the fleet from 1940. It was designed as AA gun, but it could inflict enormous losses on enemy infantry. This gun was also able to combat light tanks.
 
[EncDescription_data/units/flak/ger/flak_4_38]
Quad Flak-38 could fire up to 1800 rounds per minute. It was designed as AA gun, but it could inflict enormous losses on enemy infantry. This gun was also able to combat light tanks.
 
[EncDescription_data/units/flak/ger/flak_37_36]
Along with 20mm AA guns, 37mm Flak-18 AA gun was used against aerial targets at heights up to 2-3km and ground targets. Later modifications of 37mm AA gun were Flak-36 and Flak-43. At may 1944 there were 775 such guns in Wehrmacht and 3400 in Luftwaffe.

[EncDescription_data/units/flak/ger/flakpanzer_38t_l_(gepard)]
Development of a self-propelled anti-aircraft system on a tank chassis began in 1943, by Hitler’s order. A system designed on the basis of the Pz.Kpfw.38(t) Ausf.M chassis was among the developments. The same chassis was used in the Marder III Ausf.M tank destroyer and Bison Ausf.M self-propelled artillery. Supplies of the first self-propelled anti-aircraft systems, designated Flakpanzer 38(t) Gepard, began in November 1943. The system had basically the Pz.Kpfw.38(t) Ausf.M chassis with a 2 cm Flak 38 anti-aircraft gun in the body back. In the travelling position, the gun was covered by folding shields mounted on the sides of the cockpit. A total of 141 Flakpanzer 38(t) were manufactured.
Even though the Flakpanzer 38(t) became the second most produced German self-propelled anti-aircraft system on a tank chassis, it didn’t perform too well as a combat unit. The Flak 38 did not have enough capacity to effectively counter enemy planes, and installation of a more powerful armament was impeded by the small size of the cockpit.

[EncDescription_data/units/flak/usa/40mm_Bofors_AA_Gun]
40mm Automatic Gun M1 "Bofors" Anti-Aircraft Autocannon

The US Army placed a lot of importance on small-calibre anti-aircraft guns, most effective against fighter-bombers and dive bombers. As early as 1927, a 37mm M2A1 gun was accepted for service; however, due to the Great Depression it only went into mass production in 1938. The M2A1 remained in production until the end of WW2, but due to a list of concerns it did not fully satisfy the needs of the Army.

By 1933, the Swedish Bofors company created a very successful 40mm anti-aircraft gun after a series of modifications. The weapon quickly proved very popular, and was ordered by several different states. Bofors guns were used by the armed forces in Poland, Finland, Norway, and other countries. The weapon was tested in Britain in 1937, and was selected as the best of all existing types. The British purchased a manufacturing license, but setting up mass production proved difficult, due in no small part to the use of the metric system in Sweden, and the imperial system in Great Britain. The British version of the weapon was designated QF 40mm Mark III.

US got their Bofors guns from the UK. After a field trial, the gun was evaluated as satisfying all requirements and accepted for service. Some examples were purchased directly from Sweden, and a manufacturing license was also purchased. The main manufacturer of the US Bofors was Chrysler, whose engineers made multiple improvements to the design, mainly attempting to further streamline the manufacturing process. Serial production in the US began in 1941.

The Bofors 40mm gun was, without a doubt, a very successful weapon. It was widely used all over the world, and it inspired many related designs, such as the Soviet 37mm 61-K AAA. Bofors guns had excellent performance, high rate of fire and reliability, and were easy to use. The main shell was a high-explosive type, but an armour-piercing shell was also available, which made the gun capable of engaging armoured vehicles.

In the US Army, AAA guns such as the Bofors were mainly used within separate anti-aircraft battalions, which were attached to various divisions as needed. In this role the M1 40mm Automatic Gun was widely used in North Africa, Italy, Europe, and Pacific.

[EncDescription_data/units/flak/usa/m16_mgmc]
Work on deriving various self-propelled systems, including anti-aircraft , from the Halftrack Personnel Carrier M3 began nearly simultaneously with the vehicle’s commissioning. The first experimental vehicle of this kind was the T1E1 with a Bendix aviation ring mount. A few more experimental vehicles were designed in 1941-1942, and the T1E4 prototype was eventually developed. The vehicle carried the Maxson M33 ring mount with a coaxial Browning M2HB machine gun. In order to increase the firing angle, the sides and the back plate of the body had collapsible panels that opened while in firing position. The T1E4 was commissioned in September 1942 under the designation of Multiple Gun Motor Carriage M13. Production of the M13 MGMC was launched in January 1943, and a total of 1,100 vehicles were manufactured.
While the M13 MGMC was being prepared for mass-production, designers were working on an improved model. The T58 GMC prototype carried a Maxson M45 ring mount, on which four Browning M2HB machine-guns were mounted. The vehicle was commissioned under the designation of Multiple Gun Motor Carriage M16, and its mass-production was launched in May 1943. Nearly 2,900 M16 MGMC vehicles were manufactured, moreover, about 600 M13 MGMC pieces were adjusted to M16 standards.
The M16 MGMC was the self-propelled anti-aircraft system in the U.S. army during World War II, which saw the largest production volume. Unlike the analogous German vehicle, the Sd.Kfz.7/1, the M16 MGMC had a relatively good protection. An impressive firepower made it possible to use the system against both aerial and ground targets.

[EncDescription_data/units/flak/usa/M19_GMC]
M19 Twin 40mm Gun Motor Carriage

While the US Army was on the march during World War II, their air defense was handled by anti-aircraft self-propelled guns based on the M3 APC. These vehicles were mostly equipped with 12.7mm machine guns (or one 37mm gun alongside twin 12.7mm machine guns). But despite their advantages, these vehicles had insufficient off-road capability, short range, and low projectile force. Efforts to mount the popular 40mm Bofors gun on the APC chassis were unsuccessful.
The 40mm anti-aircraft self-propelled gun, mounted on a tank chassis, had been in the works since 1942. Initially, it used a lengthened chassis from the M5 light tank. But by mid-1943, it had become clear that the М5 was fast becoming obsolete, so on May 25 of that year, the decision was made to use the chassis of the M24 light tank instead. Work was completed by the next summer, and on June 14, 1944, the new anti-aircraft self-propelled gun was adopted as the M19. However, due to a number of delays, it only entered mass production in April of 1945.
In terms of design, the M19 was an anti-aircraft self-propelled gun with an open-top, rotating turret and twin Bofors 40mm automatic guns. The vehicle was based on the chassis of the M24 light tank, but its internal layout was quite different. The engine was in the center of the hull, and separated the driver from the rest of the crew. The turret was rotated and gun elevated by means of a hydraulic drive which provided high targeting speed. Ammunition was stored in crates around the turret. In addition, two backup gun barrels were attached to the hull in front of the turret.
Overall, the M24 was one of the best anti-aircraft self-propelled guns of World War II, rivaled only by the nonserial German Kugelblitz. All other vehicles of the same class were inferior to the M19 either in terms of the number of guns or gun caliber.
Due to delays in mass production, the M19 was never mobilized against the German forces, and only 285 units were ever made. These units, however, had a long service history with the US Army.
The M19 was in active service during the Korean War, but because the North Koreans had few attack fighters, the M19 was mainly used against ground targets. Nevertheless, the gun established a very good reputation, thanks to its high fire density and good barrel elevation, which proved critical in the mountain combat frequent in Korea.

[EncDescription_data/units/flak/uk/crusader_iii_aa_mk_ii]
Anti-Air tank Crusader III AA MK II

By 1943 it had become painfully clear that the Cruiser Tank Mk.VI Crusader could no longer perform as a front-line tank. Some of these vehicles were transformed into tractors for 17-pounder antitank guns, and at the same time, engineers began experimenting with converting the tank into a self-propelled anti-aircraft system. The first such model, designated the Cruiser Tank Mk.VI Crusader III AA MK.I, was fitted with a lightly armoured open-top turret hosting a 40mm Bofors anti-aircraft gun. Sadly, this vehicle turned out to be quite cumbersome and not very successful. Even though 215 vehicles of this type were manufactured, none would take part in combat. 

The next model, the Cruiser Tank Mk.VI Crusader III AA MK.II, was far more successful. The vehicle featured a more compact turret, which housed coaxial 20-mm Oerlikon anti-aircraft guns. The turret was roofed, with the exception of the gunner’s station. In addition to the gunner, there were two loaders in the turret. In June 1943, the first prototype of the Cruiser Tank Mk.VI Crusader III AA MK.II was assembled by Morris Motors Co., which received the mass-production order shortly thereafter. A mass-produced Cruiser Tank Mk.VI Crusader III was used as the base, and only the turret was replaced. The next tank to enter mass-production was the Cruiser Tank Mk.VI Crusader III AA MK.III, whose Т-shaped bar with the sighting mechanism was replaced with an A-shaped reticule, and whose armament was improved with the addition of a 7.7mm Vickers K machine-gun. Over 600 Crusader AA Mk.II and Mk.III tanks were manufactured.

The Cruiser Tank Mk.VI Crusader III AA family were the only tank-mounted anti-aircraft systems to see wide use by the Allies in World War II. Like the similar German self-propelled anti-aircraft systems, Cruiser Tanks Mk.VI Crusader III AA could not conduct aimed fire while on the move, but in general they were excellent vehicles. 

